Stage 1: Basic Network & Messaging (Two Devices)
1. Objective

Enable two users on two different devices (PCs, laptops, mobiles) to connect to the same Node.js server and exchange real-time chat messages over the network.

2. Scope

Run a Node.js + Socket.IO server accessible to multiple devices.

Connect two devices either via:

Local network (LAN / Wi-Fi) using server’s private IP.

Public internet using a tunneling service (ngrok, localtunnel) or cloud hosting (Heroku, Render, Railway, AWS, etc.).

Users can log in with unique usernames and chat in real-time.

3. Features
3.1 Multi-Device User Login

User provides a username.

Devices connect to the same backend server using IP/domain.

Server manages connected clients in memory.

3.2 Real-time Messaging Across Devices

Messages are delivered instantly from Device A → Device B.

Each message includes sender + timestamp.

3.3 Basic UI (same as Stage 1 Option 1)

Simple login, chat window, and message input.

4. Non-Goals (Stage 1 Option 2)

No authentication beyond username.

No end-to-end encryption.

No database (all in-memory).

No file sharing or steganography detection.

5. User Flow

Start the server on Device 1 (or cloud).

Get the server address:

For LAN → http://<local-ip>:3000 (e.g., http://192.168.1.5:3000).

For internet → use ngrok http 3000 to get a public URL (e.g., https://abcd123.ngrok.io).

Open browser on Device 1 → log in as “Alice.”

Open browser on Device 2 → log in as “Bob.”

Alice sends a message → Bob receives instantly.

Bob replies → Alice receives instantly.

6. Technical Requirements
6.1 Backend

Node.js + Express + Socket.IO.

Must listen on all network interfaces (0.0.0.0).

Example:

server.listen(3000, "0.0.0.0");

6.2 Networking

LAN setup: Both devices must be on the same Wi-Fi/router.

Internet setup: Use ngrok/localtunnel OR deploy server to cloud hosting.

6.3 Frontend

Same as Stage 1 Option 1, but connect to server via:

const socket = io("http://<server-ip>:3000"); 


or with ngrok/public domain.

7. Testing Strategy

Case 1 (LAN):

Device 1: Start server, get local IP with ipconfig (Windows) or ifconfig (Linux/Mac).

Device 2: Open http://<Device1-IP>:3000.

Verify messaging works.

Case 2 (Internet):

Run ngrok http 3000.

Device 2 (anywhere in world): open given ngrok URL.

Verify messaging works.

8. Success Criteria

✅ Two devices can connect to the same chat server.
✅ Messages are delivered instantly across devices.
✅ Chat UI remains responsive and functional.
Stage 2: File Uploads & Sharing (ngrok-enabled)
1. Objective

Enable file uploads & sharing in the chat app, with ngrok exposing your local Node.js server to the internet so two users on different devices can send text + files in real time.

2. Scope

Use Multer in Node.js to handle file uploads.

Store files locally in /uploads for now.

Generate public file URLs via ngrok tunnel.

Share these URLs in chat messages.

Display inline previews for images, download links for docs.

3. Features
3.1 File Upload

Add “Attach File” button in frontend.

User selects file → sent to backend via REST API (/upload).

3.2 File Storage

Files saved locally in /uploads.

When accessed via ngrok, URL looks like:

https://<ngrok-subdomain>.ngrok-free.app/uploads/<filename>

3.3 File Sharing

Once uploaded, backend returns the public file URL.

Socket.IO sends that URL as a chat message.

Receiver sees:

Image preview (if jpg/png/gif).

Download link (if pdf/docx/xlsx).

3.4 File Validation

Allowed types: .jpg, .jpeg, .png, .gif, .pdf, .docx, .xlsx, .txt.

File size limit: 25MB (configurable).

4. Security Considerations

Sanitize filenames (uuid instead of original names).

Prevent .exe, .js, .php from uploading.

Limit upload requests (rate-limiting middleware).

Use temporary ngrok tunnel only for dev/testing.

5. User Flow

User clicks “Upload File”.

File is validated and uploaded via API.

Backend saves file → returns public URL (ngrok + /uploads/<id>).

Chat app sends a Socket.IO message with that URL.

Other users see the file (preview or download link).

6. Technical Setup
6.1 Backend

Node.js + Express + Socket.IO

Middleware: Multer for handling uploads

Static hosting for /uploads:

app.use('/uploads', express.static('uploads'));


Return ngrok URL in response.

6.2 Frontend

Add file input (<input type="file">).

On upload success → send file URL in chat.

Show preview if image (use <img src="url" />).

Show download link for docs.

6.3 ngrok

Run server on localhost:3000.

Expose with:

ngrok http 3000


Share ngrok URL with second user (remote system).

7. Testing Strategy

Local test: Upload file → check if it appears in /uploads & URL works.

Ngrok test: Open chat on another device using ngrok URL → upload + preview/download.

File validation: Try invalid extensions → should reject.

Large files: Upload >25MB → should reject with error.

Multi-user test: User A uploads → User B sees instantly via socket event.

8. Success Criteria

✅ Users can upload files from different devices.
✅ Files accessible via ngrok public link.
✅ Images show inline preview.
✅ Docs show as download links.
✅ Validation & security checks work.